# =============================================================================
# Universal Agent Team - Cursor Rules
# =============================================================================
# Copy this file to any project root as `.cursorrules` to apply these rules
# Cursor will read it automatically and apply the rules to all AI interactions
# =============================================================================

## Project Overview
# This is a multi-agent software development setup. All AI assistants should
# follow these guidelines when helping with this codebase.

## Language & Framework Defaults
- For JavaScript/TypeScript projects, prefer TypeScript
- For Python projects, use type hints everywhere
- For APIs, prefer REST with OpenAPI documentation
- Use modern ES modules (import/export) over CommonJS (require)

## Code Quality Standards
- **DRY**: Don't repeat yourself - extract reusable functions
- **SOLID**: Follow SOLID principles, especially Single Responsibility
- **KISS**: Keep it simple - prefer straightforward solutions over clever ones
- **YAGNI**: You ain't gonna need it - don't add features until necessary

## Naming Conventions
- **Variables/functions**: camelCase (`getUserData`, `isActive`)
- **Classes/types**: PascalCase (`UserRepository`, `AuthConfig`)
- **Constants**: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (`MAX_RETRY_COUNT`, `DEFAULT_TIMEOUT`)
- **Files**: kebab-case for utilities, PascalCase for components (`date-utils.ts`, `Button.tsx`)

## Function Design
- Maximum 50 lines per function (excluding comments/docstrings)
- Maximum 3 parameters (use options object for more)
- Single responsibility - one function does one thing well
- Pure functions preferred (no side effects when possible)

## Error Handling
- Never swallow errors silently
- Always include error context for debugging
- Use custom error classes for domain-specific errors
- Validate inputs at the boundary (API routes, CLI args)

```typescript
// Good
async function fetchUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
    if (!id) throw new ArgumentError('User ID is required');
    try {
        return await userRepository.findById(id);
    } catch (error) {
        throw new DatabaseError(`Failed to fetch user ${id}`, error);
    }
}
```

## Testing
- Write tests for all business logic
- Follow AAA pattern: Arrange, Act, Assert
- Test edge cases (empty inputs, null/undefined, boundary values)
- Mock external dependencies (APIs, databases)
- Minimum 80% code coverage

```python
# Good
class TestCreateUser:
    def test_should_create_user_with_valid_data(self):
        # Arrange
        user_data = {"username": "john_doe", "email": "john@example.com"}
        
        # Act
        user = create_user(user_data)
        
        # Assert
        assert user.username == "john_doe"
        assert user.email == "john@example.com"
    
    def test_should_raise_error_for_duplicate_username(self):
        # Arrange
        create_user({"username": "john_doe", "email": "john@example.com"})
        
        # Act & Assert
        with pytest.raises(ValueError, match="Username already exists"):
            create_user({"username": "john_doe", "email": "other@example.com"})
```

## API Design
- Use RESTful conventions
- Resource names in plural nouns
- Proper HTTP status codes (200, 201, 400, 404, 500)
- Request/response in JSON
- Include API documentation (OpenAPI)

```
GET    /api/v1/users       # List users (paginated)
GET    /api/v1/users/:id   # Get single user
POST   /api/v1/users       # Create user
PUT    /api/v1/users/:id   # Update user
DELETE /api/v1/users/:id   # Delete user
```

## Database
- Use parameterized queries (never string concatenation)
- Add indexes for frequently queried fields
- Include created_at and updated_at on all tables
- Use migrations for schema changes
- Soft deletes (deleted_at) instead of hard deletes

## Security
- Never hardcode secrets or API keys
- Validate and sanitize all user inputs
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Hash passwords (bcrypt, Argon2)
- Implement rate limiting on public endpoints
- Use HTTPS in production

## Git Practices
- Follow conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `test:`
- Write descriptive commit messages
- Create focused, single-purpose commits
- Never commit .env files or secrets

## Documentation
- Update README when adding new features
- Document complex business logic with comments
- Include setup instructions for new dependencies
- Add JSDoc/docstrings to public APIs

## Review Checklist
Before merging any change, verify:
- [ ] Code compiles/runs without errors and warnings
- [ ] Tests pass locally
- [ ] No linting errors
- [ ] No console.log/print statements in production code
- [ ] Error handling is in place
- [ ] Input validation is present
- [ ] No hardcoded secrets
- [ ] Documentation updated
- [ ] No unnecessary files committed
